definition(inner product) #ip {
A map $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle: V \times V \to \mathbb{R}$ that is bilinear,
symmetric, and positive-definite.
}

theorem(Cauchy-Schwarz) #cs {
For all vectors $u, v$ in an inner product space:

$$$
|\langle u, v \rangle| \le \|u\| \|v\|
$$$
}

proof {
Expand $\|u - tv\|^2 \ge 0$ for a real parameter $t$, then choose $t$ to
minimize the right-hand side. The result follows from the discriminant of
the resulting quadratic being non-positive.
}

remark {
The inequality @cs follows directly from the positive-definiteness required
of the inner product @ip.
}

